  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1155 AM CDT Fri Aug 7 2026\n\nLate morning water vapor satellite imagery and RAP upper air \nanalysis reveal a ~597-dm ridge is in place over the Desert \nSouthwest, with troughing over south-central Canada into the upper \nMidwest resulting in northwesterly flow atop the central plains. At \nthe surface, weak low pressure was located over northwest KS, \nsupporting breezy winds across our area. Through the remainder of \nthe day, short range ensembles agree the upper ridge will slightly \nbuild to near ~599-dm. Under the increasing subsidence, 850-mb \ntemperatures will warm by 2-3 degrees C, which will translate to a \nsubtle uptick in afternoon temperatures today with highs in the \nupper 90s/low 100s. DNVA ahead of the ridge should also inhibit \nconvective activity, although a stray thunderstorm or two cannot be \nruled out, especially after last night's severe thunderstorms that \ndeveloped in the absence of synoptic support.\n\nDaytime Saturday, ensembles indicate little to no change will occur \nin the overall upper level pattern, with ridging to our west \nremaining dominant. The only noteworthy difference will be lee \ncyclogenesis spread out over eastern WY/CO and adjacent areas \nleading to gusty southerly winds sustained in the 10-20 mph range \ngusting to 30 mph. Continued strong subsidence and solar insolation \nwill foster another small increase in afternoon temperatures with \nafternoon highs reaching into the low 100s.\n\nSunday through the end of the period, medium range ensembles agree \nthe upper level ridge to our west will begin to break down and \nweaken, which will engender a gradual cooling trend across southwest \nKS as well as a return of appreciable precipitation chances. NBM \nhighs on Sunday are in the 100-105 range, but will inch downward to \nthe upper 80s by the end of the business week. NBM also highlights \nWednesday and Thursday evenings as the most likely opportunities for \nwidespread precipitation as pops rise into the chance (25-54%) and \nlow-end likely (55-74%) categories. These will be welcome changes to \nthe central plains which have been mired in very hot and dry \ntemperatures for several weeks.",
